        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces every nan (\"not a number\") and every infinity with an ordinary number of your choosing, leaving all the other values as they are.\n\nOne nan or infinity can wreck everything after it: a plot loses its range, a sum or mean turns to nan, a filter remembers the bad value for ever. Put np.nan_to_num in front of anything that cannot cope - a quick, blunt repair. When the gaps matter, np.isnan or np.isfinite will show you where they are first.\n\nHere all three default to 0. NumPy's own np.nan_to_num turns infinity into the largest number a float can hold instead, which is rarely useful in a patch; set posinf and neginf to large values if you want that.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nan_to_num <nan: float>\nDefault: nan 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.nan_to_num -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is repaired and sent. Whole-number arrays cannot hold nan or infinity and go through unchanged.\n\nnan (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat each nan becomes.\n\nposinf (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat inf (positive infinity) becomes.\n\nneginf (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat -inf becomes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe input with nan and infinity replaced, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0 becomes 1 0 3 0 0 0 with the defaults. The second node marks the gaps instead: -1 for nan, 999 for inf and -999 for -inf. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; change the numbers in the second node.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isnan and np.isfinite find the gaps rather than filling them. np.clip limits ordinary numbers to a range (it leaves nan as nan).",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces every nan (\"not a number\") and every infinity with an ordinary number of your choosing, leaving all the other values as they are.\n\nOne nan or infinity can wreck everything after it: a plot loses its range, a sum or mean turns to nan, a filter remembers the bad value for ever. Put np.nan_to_num in front of anything that cannot cope - a quick, blunt repair. When the gaps matter, np.isnan or np.isfinite will show you where they are first.\n\nHere all three default to 0. NumPy's own np.nan_to_num turns infinity into the largest number a float can hold instead, which is rarely useful in a patch; set posinf and neginf to large values if you want that.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nan_to_num <nan: float>\nDefault: nan 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.nan_to_num -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is repaired and sent. Whole-number arrays cannot hold nan or infinity and go through unchanged.\n\nnan (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat each nan becomes.\n\nposinf (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat inf (positive infinity) becomes.\n\nneginf (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat -inf becomes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe input with nan and infinity replaced, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0 becomes 1 0 3 0 0 0 with the defaults. The second node marks the gaps instead: -1 for nan, 999 for inf and -999 for -inf. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; change the numbers in the second node.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isnan and np.isfinite find the gaps rather than filling them. np.clip limits ordinary numbers to a range (it leaves nan as nan)."
        },
